(LST2-36 — "the first append determines the list's element type for
 printing" (old manual line 2753) is NOT what happens: every element renders
 by its own runtime tag whatever the first append was, in both directions
 and whether the list started empty or from a literal. See Discrepancy 5.
 Also covers: LST2-37 (append works with integers, texts, booleans,
 variables and expressions), LST2-38 (the append-integers example),
 LST2-40 (the append-from-variables example), LST2-41 (the
 append-in-a-loop example, whose value is an expression).
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox LST2-36.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   ["text first", 42]
   [42, "then text"]
   [1, 2, "late text"]
   [10, "ten", 1, 42, 84]
   [1, 4, 9, 16, 25]
)
a list called sayings is [].
append "text first" to sayings.
append 42 to sayings.
print sayings.

a list called tallies is [].
append 42 to tallies.
append "then text" to tallies.
print tallies.

a list called seeded is [1, 2].
append "late text" to seeded.
print seeded.

a list called assorted is [].
append 10 to assorted.
append "ten" to assorted.
append yes to assorted.
a number called seed is 42.
append seed to assorted.
append seed multiply 2 to assorted.
print assorted.

a list called squares is [].
a number called step is 1.
While step is less than or equal to 5,
  append step multiply step to squares,
  increment step.

print squares.
